WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.332 --> 00:00:12.790
JASON JOEL: Hi, welcome to Next.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:15.040
My name is Jason Joel,
and I'm a Product Manager

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.660
with Google Cloud.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.960
I'm presenting here today
with my peer, Sai Gopalan.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.940
And in this session,
we will deep

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:25.630
dive into the tools and services
to accelerate your migration

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.870
to Google Cloud.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.640
There are several
motivators that

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.560
lead customers to
migrate to the cloud

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.230
and modernize their
applications and infrastructure.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:37.750
Many customers begin by
wanting to use the cloud

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.000
to increase the
speed of innovation

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.170
and address customer
requirements faster.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.180
But often, the
project is initially

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.060
justified by an
impending financial need,

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:51.480
like an upcoming hardware
refresh, a significant business

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.230
event, such as an acquisition,
or an upcoming deadline,

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.970
like an expiring
data center contract.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.770
But it turns out migrating
and modernization

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.360
to the cloud is hard.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.220
Enterprise migration
projects often

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.660
face delays and go over-budget.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.780
But this is typically
because customers do not

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:17.635
understand the applications
that they are trying to migrate.

00:01:17.635 --> 00:01:19.010
They do not have
a good plan that

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.200
includes moving
all of their data,

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.135
and they generally
underestimate the scale.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:30.660
At Google, we have a proven
four-step methodology

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.290
to help our customers and
meet them where they are,

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:39.610
and accelerate their path to
the Google Cloud platform.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:41.620
Actually, it's not
four linear steps,

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.840
but this is more
of a process that

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.060
will be followed iteratively
throughout the migration

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:46.560
journey.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.130
The first step is
the assessment phase.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:54.780
And for this assessment
phase, Google

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.840
has a tool called StratoZone.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.470
And StratoZone enables
you to do five things

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.600
as part of your assessment.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.437
First and foremost, it helps
you discover what workloads,

00:02:07.437 --> 00:02:09.229
applications, and
virtual machines that you

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.150
have that you want to
migrate to the Cloud,

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:15.380
and build an inventory as a
place to start, a baseline.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:18.110
The second thing it
does is it evaluates

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:21.320
each of your workloads for
its suitability for the cloud,

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.360
gives you a Cloud
Fit Score, and helps

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.120
you identify what
remediations you may need

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.160
to take in order to move it.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.680
The third part of the
StratoZone process

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.290
is optimizing your
consumption plan

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.240
and identifying where
are the best places

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.570
to move a particular
application to Google.

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:42.780
Google offers many services,
and it's not always obvious

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:48.100
where the best
location is to move

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.920
and would give you the
biggest impact for your money.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.410
The fourth step is
actually providing you

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:59.390
with an ROI, a real plan, a
return on your investment paper

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.250
that you can share
with your enterprise,

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.950
and share with your executives
and your stakeholders

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.422
to get an idea of what the
return on their investment

00:03:06.422 --> 00:03:07.880
will be, and the
benefits they will

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.220
gain from moving to the cloud.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.400
And the last step of the
StratoZone consumption plan

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.280
is developing this to
provide you with insights

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:18.030
into what you should
do first, second,

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.748
and third in your journey.

00:03:20.748 --> 00:03:23.290
Let's look at some of the key
features of the StratoZone Data

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:25.290
Collector in itself.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.570
It's agentless, and
it does not require

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:31.290
you to install any software in
each of your virtual machines

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.780
or on your physical machines.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.090
All the information
you send is encrypted

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:39.030
in transit and at rest.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:41.760
It's very lightweight, so
it doesn't put any burden

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:43.470
on your environment.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.300
And in fact, the data
that it transfers

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:49.550
is roughly less than
400 kilobytes in size.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:51.800
You have the option
to anonymize any data

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.720
that you think might
be sensitive, including

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.450
IP addresses and machine names.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:58.460
And it's overall not intrusive.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:00.320
You can control the
scope of the discovery

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.630
that you run from a
single application,

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:06.426
to a single data center, to a
multi-data center or migration.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.390
One of my peers,
[INAUDIBLE],, will now

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:15.598
give you a short demo
on how StratoZone works.

00:04:15.598 --> 00:04:17.010
SPEAKER 2: StratoZone
assessments

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.350
can scale globally with
multiple data collections

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.500
feeding data from various
customer locations.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.170
The assessment relies on
inventory and telemetry data,

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.660
and is conducted within
the StratoZone portal.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.390
All data is encrypted
in transit and at rest.

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.430
No actual business
confidential data

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:36.540
is collected, such as data
classified as PCI or HIPAA.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.370
Once the assessment
collection has begun,

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:40.410
you will be able to see
the results populate

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.390
within the StratoZone portal.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:44.730
Results include overall
utilization levels,

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:47.640
age of the machines, details
about operating systems,

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.590
and cloud migration
compatibility scoring,

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:51.420
among others.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.000
You will also be able to
review detailed inventory

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.970
in each machine's detail,
such as its name, IP address,

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:58.860
and configuration specs.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:00.510
You will be able to
further drill down

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.580
into utilization,
installed software,

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.410
and running processes.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:05.910
Network connectivity
maps will be

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.670
available for any point-to-point
detected connections.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:10.530
They can be viewed
in graphical form,

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:13.320
as well as in the table form,
which represents details

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.170
about source and target
IPs, ports, and processes,

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:18.130
such as SQL Server,
in this case,

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:20.460
responsible for the
connection socket.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.110
Detailed pricing
views are available

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:25.110
for each assessed location,
application group, or migration

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:25.860
group.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.410
Within the groups, machines
are evaluated individually

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:30.030
based on the
recorded utilization

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.400
and user-defined
right-sizing profiles.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.140
Each machine's
cost breakdown can

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:36.750
be viewed in detail to
determine compute, storage,

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.190
and egress costs.

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:41.160
Pricing can be generated for a
variety of different catalogs,

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.790
including GC predefined types,
custom shapes, sole tenancy,

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.970
and [? GCV. ?] Committed
and sustained discounts

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:49.770
are included in the calculation.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:52.800
Custom discounting can also
be defined by the user.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.220
Pricing can also be tailored
to relevant GCP regions.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.650
StratoZone also stores
industry benchmark costs

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:00.930
for operating
on-premises data centers,

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:04.110
which can be used in
comparisons to devise the most

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.150
suitable pricing scenarios.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.550
At any point in the
assessment, a customizable deck

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:11.250
can be generated in the
reporting dashboard.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:13.920
It can be fully customized to
include the relevant workload

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.610
groups, optimizations, pricing
catalogs, and discounts.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.290
This assessment
summary can serve

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:22.530
as a leave-behind customer
deliverable and a proposal.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.420
This deck will cover
the assessment approach

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:26.970
and findings, along with
pricing information.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.920
Findings represented
for all assets

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:31.470
or broken down by
user-configurable groups.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.930
Solutions such as
databases and app engines

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:37.230
will be identified, along
with potential GCP targets.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:41.777
TCO reports will be generated to
provide meaningful comparisons.

00:06:41.777 --> 00:06:43.860
JASON JOEL: After completing
the assessment stage,

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.580
you enter the planning phase.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:48.410
Google has a pricing
methodology that

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:53.000
helps make initial progress
quickly, reduce risk, and stay

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.400
on budget.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:58.640
StratoZone can help you arrange
migration candidates in order

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:02.300
of priority, based on business
impact and the migration

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:05.020
effort that's required.

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:08.620
A large migration
will have many waves,

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:11.890
with velocity increasing
as the migrations progress

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:13.900
and experience in the
organization is gained.

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:19.340
By categorizing your workloads
in the waves based on these two

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:23.208
parameters, you can iterate,
learn from those iterations,

00:07:23.208 --> 00:07:24.500
and then optimize your process.

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:32.150
With StratoZone's
Cloud Readiness Report,

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:34.830
you can see which assets
are high-, medium-,

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:38.880
and low-fit for
moving to the Cloud.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.410
This is based on characteristics
such as capacity, performance,

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:48.090
age, operating system, and
other machine-based attributes.

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:52.640
Now Sai will explain
the tools that Google

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:57.260
has to help you with the
Migrate Phase of your journey.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:07:58.590
SAI GOPALAN: Thank you, Jason.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:07:58.920
Hi.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.200
I'm Sai, and I'm a Product
Manager with Google Cloud.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:02.860
Let's jump right in.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:04.470
When we look at
migrations, customers

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:07.110
have specific paths to
migrating to Google Cloud.

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:09.090
Each migration path
has an increasing level

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:11.280
of transformation,
and therefore change.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.900
And we have a number of tools
that can help you with that.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:16.410
For example, in addition
to moving your specialized

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:20.700
workloads or VMs to the
Google Cloud bare metal

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:23.430
Solution, or Google Cloud VMware
Engine, or Compute Engine,

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:28.140
or to GKE, you may want to move
your unstructured archival data

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:31.020
from an on-premises file
system to the cloud.

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:32.700
And we offer a few
options, including

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:36.179
a fully-online storage transfer
service and a dedicated

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:39.630
transfer appliance that can be
shipped to your data center.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:41.940
Let's look at a few
tools in more detail

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:43.110
in the subsequent section.

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:47.830
The first tool is Migrate
for Compute Engine

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:49.270
that's delivered as a service.

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:53.260
Our latest version delivers a
Google-managed cloud service

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.270
that enables simple,
friction-less,

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:58.690
and large-scale enterprise
migration of virtual machines

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:02.650
to Compute Engine with
minimal downtime and risk.

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:04.793
Hundreds of VMs,
fast-to-start, this

00:09:04.793 --> 00:09:06.460
is a replication-based
tool, which means

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:08.590
that it's predictable as well.

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:11.620
API driven and integrated
into your Google Cloud Console

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:15.920
for ease of use, you can do
your end-to-end migration

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:17.900
through the Google
Cloud Console.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:20.060
This service uses
agentless replication

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:22.190
to copy data without
manual intervention

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:24.360
and without VPN requirements.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:28.280
It also enables you to launch
non-disruptive validations

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:30.980
of your VMs prior to cutover.

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:33.170
You can rapidly migrate
a single application

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:36.530
or execute a sprint with 100
systems using Migration Groups

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:39.740
with confidence.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:41.630
There are six easy steps.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:43.220
You enable your API.

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:45.320
You deploy a
migration connector,

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:47.480
on-board and
initiate replication,

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:50.870
set your VM target, perform
your test, and cut over.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:53.270
Users can test their
workloads pre-cutover

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:54.980
by creating clones
of the workload

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:57.260
from the replicated
data in the cloud

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:59.780
without disrupting
the source workload.

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:02.990
It also adapts the migrating
workloads automatically

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:04.850
to run natively in the cloud.

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:08.180
And it includes network
setting and installation

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:10.880
of cloud agents for seamless
integration with Cloud Services

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:12.800
post-migration as well.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:16.250
It supports migrating
workloads to different target

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.080
configurations on the fly.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:20.480
For example, users can
test-clone a workload

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:24.530
in project A while doing a
cutover of the same workload

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:28.010
to project B. Let us
now hear from Rackspace

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:30.402
and their experience
with this tool.

