00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:23.970
Now, Migrate for Compute
Engine enables users

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:26.940
to group VMs based on
their needs and scenarios,

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:30.370
and execute migration
operations on a group level,

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:32.190
as well as a subset
of the group.

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:36.270
This flexibility allows for you
to edit the migration details

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:38.790
for one or many of
the VMs in the group,

00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:43.140
and perform replication, test
cloning, and cutover, as well.

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:47.000
Let us now look at
the lifecycle of a VM.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:49.610
It starts with
on-boarding the VM, which

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:52.430
means telling the tool that
it's ready to start replication.

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:55.790
And once you start replication,
an initial sync happens.

00:11:55.790 --> 00:12:00.330
All of this without
disruption to the source VMs.

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:03.180
This is followed by continuous
replication thereafter.

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:08.800
You can also perform
non-disruptive testing

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:10.030
with test cloning.

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:12.520
There is no disruption
to on-premises VM.

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:15.520
And it's always a good idea
to validate that a VM will

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:18.532
run correctly in the cloud.

00:12:18.532 --> 00:12:20.640
Finally, you cut
over to the Cloud

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:23.230
during a scheduled
maintenance window.

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:25.800
This is when the source
VM is powered off,

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:28.890
a final sync of the VM
data is done to the Cloud,

00:12:28.890 --> 00:12:31.840
and that's it.

