00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:54.680
and how you can perform
bulk operations with groups.

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:57.590
Here we see the main
dashboard of Migrate

00:12:57.590 --> 00:13:00.020
for Compute Engine that's
completely integrated

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:01.640
into our Cloud console.

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:05.510
It shows an overview of
the migrations, the events,

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:08.960
the sources, the
groups, and the target.

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:14.930
Let's go to the Sources tab.

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:16.970
In the Sources tab,
I can see that I

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:20.090
have multiple on-prem
data centers that

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:22.220
act as sources of
virtual machines

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:23.750
that I want to migrate from.

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:30.530
For each one of these
sources, I can actually

00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:35.940
see that I have the VM
inventory cached in the system.

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:39.950
This allows you to execute
end-to-end migrations

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:41.525
without ever
leaving the console.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:49.360
Migrate for Compute Engine
uses replication to migrate

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:52.960
the virtual machine's data
over to the destination,

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:54.870
or the target.

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:57.780
As you can see over here,
there are virtual machines

00:13:57.780 --> 00:13:59.970
that are ready for replication.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:02.530
There are virtual
machines that are active,

00:14:02.530 --> 00:14:05.910
which means that there is
periodic replication that's

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:07.590
ongoing in the background.

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:11.300
And there is a virtual machine
that's already been cutover.

00:14:11.300 --> 00:14:15.190
Let's go into the Migration tab.

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:19.990
This is the place where I do
all of my migration operations.

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:25.240
I can see the status of
all my migrating VMs.

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:29.710
I can go into the
Info panel and edit

00:14:29.710 --> 00:14:35.350
the details of my
migration virtual machine.

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:37.930
Here I've selected w12core.

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:42.310
And what I can do over here
is I can edit the landing

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:44.680
zone of the migrating VM.

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:53.420
And I can also select a VM and
perform migration operations.

00:14:53.420 --> 00:15:00.940
Actions include starting
the replication,

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:03.370
pausing the replication,
resuming the replication,

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:06.510
finalizing the replication.

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:10.530
It also includes testing
the virtual machine

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:12.870
with no impact to the
source VM in order

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:16.450
to validate that the workload in
the cloud is running correctly.

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:19.680
And finally, cutting over
with minimal downtime.

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:26.520
Many customers also want
to migrate virtual machines

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:28.130
in bulk.

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:31.790
The Group feature
allows you to do that.

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:36.230
Here I see that I have actually
grouped three virtual machines

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:39.660
in a group called myapp-1.

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:44.430
Groups enable you to
execute operations in bulk,

00:15:44.430 --> 00:15:48.870
such as bulk editing of
migration details, test

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:52.050
clone, and cutover.

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:58.320
Here I can see that I can select
all my VMs or a subset of them,

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:03.000
and perform bulk editing
of migration details

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:06.030
on all of them.

00:16:06.030 --> 00:16:10.020
I can also do test
cloning and cutover

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:15.610
for all of these at one
go, or a subset of these.

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:19.800
This brings us to the end
of a quick overview demo.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.550
Now many customers want to
migrate their VMware workloads

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:25.310
to a native vSphere environment
running in the cloud

00:16:25.310 --> 00:16:27.830
to increase their
agility and reduce costs.

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:30.260
Google Cloud VMware
Engine solves this problem

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:33.440
and gives you a fast-forward
to your journey to the cloud.

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:35.990
It is one of the fastest
ways to migrate your VMware

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:37.580
applications to the cloud.

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:41.060
And VMware HCX is the
tool that enables this.

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:43.820
It's provided as part
of VMware Engine,

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:46.310
and it abstracts the
underlying infrastructure

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:49.310
and offers many
options, including

00:16:49.310 --> 00:16:53.030
hot, cold, and warm
migration options in bulk.

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:58.860
Now, many customers also want
to fast-forward to modernizing

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:00.210
their applications.

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:03.810
With Migrate for Anthos,
you can transform your VMs

00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:06.720
to containers that
is managed by Anthos.

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:10.140
With migrate for GKE, you
can transform your VMs

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:14.609
to containers that's managed
by GKE in either Standard Mode,

00:17:14.609 --> 00:17:16.569
or Autopilot Mode.

00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:18.690
Finally, you can
transform your VMs

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:21.089
to containers that's
managed by Cloud Run

00:17:21.089 --> 00:17:25.140
so you can get started with the
server-less architecture right

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:25.890
off the bat.

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:32.040
Once more, this happens in
a simple six-step process.

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:35.160
You qualify a VM, configure
the processing cluster

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:38.370
to examine the source VM,
and create a migration plan

00:17:38.370 --> 00:17:40.290
that you then review
and customize.

00:17:40.290 --> 00:17:42.210
Then you use the
processing cluster

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:44.380
to simply execute
the migration plan,

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:46.410
and generate your
deployment YAMLs,

00:17:46.410 --> 00:17:48.960
your container images,
your data volumes.

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:51.300
You verify that the workload
is migrated correctly

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:53.850
by running some tests, and
finally, deploy the container

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:56.310
artifacts to your testing,
staging, and production

00:17:56.310 --> 00:17:57.210
clusters.

00:17:57.210 --> 00:18:01.230
You can migrate from VMware
on-premises, AWS, Azure,

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:02.295
or even within GCE.

00:18:06.680 --> 00:18:08.990
Now once you migrated,
you want to be

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:11.270
able to keep optimizing
your environment.

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:13.730
We have a tool called
VM Manager that

00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:16.460
allows you to manage
large fleets of VMs

00:18:16.460 --> 00:18:18.200
running Windows or Linux.

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:22.820
You can manage your OS patches,
perform compliance reporting

00:18:22.820 --> 00:18:26.510
on OS patches, install, remove,
and maintain agents, apps,

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:28.730
and configurations,
or in other words,

00:18:28.730 --> 00:18:31.070
manage your OS
configurations, and also use

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:34.100
this service to collect
runtime OS information.

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:39.380
We also have a technology
called Activist Assist.

00:18:39.380 --> 00:18:41.030
Now, Activist Assist
is a portfolio

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:44.030
of tools that uses data,
intelligence, and machine

00:18:44.030 --> 00:18:46.220
learning to reduce
cloud complexity

00:18:46.220 --> 00:18:50.030
and administrative toil, making
it easy for you to optimize

00:18:50.030 --> 00:18:51.530
your cloud environment.

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:54.950
Whether you're interested in
security, cost, networking,

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:59.120
compute, data, or operations, we
have the intelligence for you.

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:01.340
Some new Active Assist
features include

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:05.900
idle resource recommendations,
compute committed use discount

00:19:05.900 --> 00:19:07.160
recommendations, and more.

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:11.900
So what are you waiting for?

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:13.990
You can start with a
quick assessment that's

00:19:13.990 --> 00:19:15.790
offered at no additional cost.

00:19:15.790 --> 00:19:17.770
You will receive a
detailed inventory

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:21.040
of your virtual and physical
server assets and database

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:23.350
estate, and a
customized total cost

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:25.660
of ownership report for
moving to the cloud,

00:19:25.660 --> 00:19:29.900
as well as a detailed
end-to-end migration plan.

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:32.180
This brings us to the
end of this presentation.

00:19:32.180 --> 00:19:34.700
On behalf of Jason
and I, we thank you

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:36.880
for watching this session.

